 "permalink": "https://stackoverflow.com/questions/2662652/why-would-i-get-a-bus-error-or-segmentation-fault-when-calling-free-normally",
 "body_html": "<p>I have a very simple test program, running on Solaris 5.8:</p>\n\n<pre><code>#include &lt;stdio.h&gt;\n#include &lt;stdlib.h&gt;\n\nint main(void)\n{\n    char *paths;\n    paths = getenv(\"PATH\");\n    printf(\"Paths: %s\\n\", paths);\n\n    free(paths); // this causes a bus error\n    return 0;\n}\n</code></pre>\n\n<p>If I don't call <code>free()</code> at the end, it displays the message fine and exits.  If I include the <code>free()</code> call, it crashes with a bus error.  I've had other calls to <code>free()</code>, in other programs, cause segmentation faults as well.</p>\n\n<p>Even if I allocate the memory for <code>*paths</code> myself, <code>free()</code> will cause a bus error.  Is there some reason trying to free up the memory is causing a crash?</p>\n",
 "passes": [
  "main"
 ],
 "data_source": "so_main_cached (2026-10-05 collection)",
 "in_main_retained_480": false,
 "in_main_nonNA_coded_346": false,
 "in_offcwe_nonNA_coded": true,
 "events": [
  {
   "post_type": "answer",
   "post_id": 2662657,
   "parent_id": 2662652,
   "author_user_id": 224671,
   "author_display_name": "kennytm",
   "author_id_missing": false,
   "role": "responder",
   "is_accepted": true,
   "permalink": "https://stackoverflow.com/a/2662657",
   "score": 5,
   "created_utc": "2010-04-18T15:04:46Z",
   "last_edit_utc": null,
   "last_activity_utc": "2010-04-18T15:04:46Z",
   "license": "CC BY-SA 2.5",
   "body_html": "<p>Because you're not supposed to <code>free</code> the value returned from <code>getenv()</code> (it's a static variable). </p>\n"
  },
  {
   "post_type": "answer",
   "post_id": 2662660,
   "parent_id": 2662652,
   "author_user_id": null,
   "author_display_name": "anon",
   "author_id_missing": true,
   "role": "responder",
   "is_accepted": false,
   "permalink": "https://stackoverflow.com/a/2662660",
   "score": 1,
   "created_utc": "2010-04-18T15:04:55Z",
   "last_edit_utc": null,
   "last_activity_utc": "2010-04-18T15:04:55Z",
   "license": "CC BY-SA 2.5",
   "body_html": "<p>The memory returned by getenv() is not allocated by malloc() and should therefore not be freed by free(). In general, none of the C standard library functions that are not part of the malloc() family should be freed using free(), or in fact freed at all.</p>\n"
  },
  {
   "post_type": "answer",
   "post_id": 2662663,
   "parent_id": 2662652,
   "author_user_id": 39138,
   "author_display_name": "Roland Rabien",
   "author_id_missing": false,
   "role": "responder",
   "is_accepted": false,
   "permalink": "https://stackoverflow.com/a/2662663",
   "score": 1,
   "created_utc": "2010-04-18T15:05:27Z",
   "last_edit_utc": null,
   "last_activity_utc": "2010-04-18T15:05:27Z",
   "license": "CC BY-SA 2.5",
   "body_html": "<blockquote>\n  <p>The string pointed by the pointer\n  returned by this function shall not be\n  modified by the program.</p>\n</blockquote>\n\n<p>It's not your memory to free.</p>\n"
  },
  {
   "post_type": "comment",
   "post_id": 2680149,
   "parent_id": 2662657,
   "parent_type": "answer",
   "author_user_id": null,
